ges. The eventual prototype may emit a machine-readable comparison report, but graphlite discover/check behavior remains unchanged. Any future production adapter must preserve nodes.qualified_name, nodes.trait_impl, and rustdoc-sourced IMPL_TRAIT edge meaning.","open_questions":"Which LSP request combination can produce a complete, bounded inventory for private workspace items? Can documentSymbol hierarchy reconstruct rustdoc-equivalent qualified names across out-of-line modules and re-exports? Can implementation responses enumerate all relevant IMPL_TRAIT edges, including blanket and cross-crate cases? What readiness condition is both bounded and correlated with probe completeness when quiescent never fires? What parity threshold and fallback policy justify a later production PDR?","problem":"PDR-0002 Phase 1 keeps rustdoc JSON as the production source for Rust qualified names and trait-implementation edges, while the existing rust-analyzer client could potentially reduce nightly/toolchain and rustdoc-schema fragility. The prior spike found that the current quiescent readiness signal did not fire within 170 seconds, so adopting LSP sourcing without a stronger validation plan would risk incomplete graph data.","proposed_design":"Build a standalone prototype or test harness around the existing LSP framing/client concepts, without wiring it into discover. Start rust-analyzer with serverStatusNotification enabled, collect serverStatus health/quiescent and $/progress events, then issue bounded probes after readiness. For qualified names, compare per-file textDocument/documentSymbol hierarchy and reconstructed module paths against rustdoc/tree-sitter ground truth; do not use workspace/symbol as the complete inventory because rust-analyzer documents fuzzy search, type-oriented defaults, workspace/dependency scopes, and a result limit. For trait edges, compare textDocument/implementation or equivalent implementation responses for representative traits, types, blanket impls, associated methods, and cross-crate cases. Run repeated cold-cache and warm-cache trials on a representative multi-crate workspace. A production proposal may only proceed if all signals meet parity thresholds, readiness is bounded and repeatable, and an LSP failure falls back to the existing rustdoc result or per-crate skip without corrupting committed data.","requirements":"Evaluate, without changing production behavior, whether rust-analyzer can provide qualified_name and IMPL_TRAIT fidelity equal to or better than rustdoc on representative single- and multi-crate workspaces. Establish a repeatable readiness protocol, preserve per-crate warn-and-skip semantics, measure cold and warm wall time, and define explicit fallback behavior. Visibility remains tree-sitter-owned and is not part of this phase.","resulting_decisions":"Create an evaluation-only Phase 2 record. Do not modify src/lsp_client.rs, do not change discover/check, and do not imply that Phase 2 production implementation has begun. The next authorized step is a standalone prototype and benchmark evidence; production integration requires a follow-up implementation record after the exit criteria are met.","risks":"The serverStatus signal may be emitted before all useful semantic queries are stable, or may remain quiescent=false indefinitely on valid projects. LSP inventory APIs may omit private or dependency symbols, impose limits, or return locations without enough path context. Implementation requests may require one request per trait/type and become slower than rustdoc. Rust-analyzer version drift can change experimental behavior. A prototype that reports only favorable hover/documentSymbol examples would produce false confidence, so acceptance must be corpus-wide and failure-injected."}', '2026-08-21T19:35:36.809918915+00:00', NULL, 'status changed to review');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('RFC-0001', 1, '{"alternatives":"Keep the current fixed compile-time language list (what actually shipped): simpler, fully typed, no runtime grammar-loading machinery, but requires a release to add a language and cannot handle a codebase mixing an unsupported language gracefully beyond skip-with-warning.","constraints":"No hard failure on a missing grammar crate for a detected language -- log and skip. Detection must not require the project to build.","motivation":"The current language list (Rust, TypeScript, JavaScript, Svelte, HTML, CSS) is fixed at compile time, one tree-sitter grammar crate per language, hardcoded in language.rs. GRAPHLITE-SPEC.xml (committed alongside the v0.2 LSP-enrichment release) proposed replacing this with runtime grammar discovery: no assumption about which languages are supported ahead of time, detection instead driven by a three-tier heuristic (file extension, then shebang line, then actual parse-attempt validation with grammar-family fallback).","non_goals":"The spec does not propose changing the indexing/query pipeline itself (tree-sitter parse -> symbols/edges -> SQLite -> XML output) -- only how the set of active languages is determined and reported.","outcome":"Not implemented. Verified directly against the current codebase: `graphlite discover --help` / `capabilities --json` show no `--list-grammars`, `--dry-run`, or `--grammar-debug` flags (discover currently takes no flags at all); language.rs implements a fixed extension-to-language match, not runtime grammar scanning; no `languages` table exists in the current schema. `git log --follow` shows this file was added in the same commit as v0.2''s LSP enrichment work and has not been touched since -- consistent with a proposal that was written, not pursued, and not since revisited. Recorded here as draft, not accepted or withdrawn, since no explicit review or withdrawal decision is evidenced; a maintainer should either move this forward or formally withdraw it so the record reflects an actual decision rather than an open, silently-abandoned one.","problem":"A fixed, compile-time language list means adding a language requires a new graphlite release. The proposal''s premise is that a codebase''s actual language mix should be discovered, not pre-declared, and that detection should degrade gracefully (parseable_unknown, unparseable-metadata-only) rather than simply skipping unrecognized extensions.","proposal":"Runtime grammar discovery as described under `scope`, with the CLAUDE_DISCOVERY_PROTOCOL section of the spec explicitly written as agent-facing guidance (\"never assume languages, wait for discover output first\").","questions_for_review":"Was this proposal ever formally reviewed, or written and left in draft? -- Left in draft: this record backfills it honestly rather than assuming acceptance. Does the current fixed-list implementation already satisfy the real need well enough that the added runtime-discovery complexity isn''t worth it? -- Open; no evidence either way was found in this codebase''s history. Is `tier3_parsing_validation`''s try-then-fallback-grammar-family approach (e.g. attempting a rust parse on an unrecognized C-like file) something the tree-sitter query layer can actually support today, or does it require substantial new query-file infrastructure per grammar family?","scope":"As specified in GRAPHLITE-SPEC.xml: extension-to-language mapping with a shebang-line fallback, a parse-attempt validation tier with grammar-family fallback ordering (e.g. typescript tried before javascript for .js files), a new `languages` SQLite table recording per-language file counts/parse success/confidence, and CLI surface additions (`discover --list-grammars`, `--dry-run`, `--grammar-debug`) plus a `symbols --language` filter over the dynamically discovered set."}', '2026-08-21T15:46:21.981040956+00:00', NULL, 'created');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('RFC-0002', 1, '{"alternatives":"(a) Parallelize enrich_crates as-is (rayon .par_iter() over crates, mirroring discover.rs) -- lowest-risk, addresses the concretely-felt slowness directly, does not touch the JSON-parsing or dual-identity-space concerns. (b) Adopt the rustdoc-types crate for typed parsing -- reduces schema-drift maintenance burden, does not address serial execution or the tree-sitter/rustdoc identity-reconciliation fragility (span_file_match_keys'' four match-form normalization, four dedicated tests). (c) Investigate replacing rustdoc JSON with the existing LSP client (lsp_client.rs) as the single semantic-enrichment source -- would remove one of three code-understanding mechanisms entirely and might sidestep the identity-reconciliation problem if the LSP path can address symbols the same way tree-sitter does, but README.md now states `rename` (the LSP client''s current sole consumer) is deprecated in favor of editor-native LSP rename, while main.rs and `--help` still actively dispatch Commands::Rename -- an unresolved, unverified-by-this-record inconsistency that makes (c) impossible to responsibly recommend without maintainer clarification first: leaning into a component that may itself be scheduled for removal would be building on ground that isn''t settled. These are not mutually exclusive -- (a) and (b) are compatible with either doing or not doing (c).","constraints":"Rustdoc-derived data (qualified_name, visibility, trait_impl/IMPL_TRAIT edges) must remain available at the same fidelity for Rust projects; this is a performance/robustness/architecture redesign, not a feature reduction. Whatever replaces or restructures the current mechanism must still degrade to a per-crate warning-and-skip on failure, matching the existing failure_modes documented in PDR-0001, not a hard stop for the whole index.","motivation":"Rustdoc-based enrichment (qualified names, visibility, trait-impl edges) is the one stage of the indexing pipeline that is felt, directly and repeatedly, as slow -- reported independently by this project''s own maintainer, not just observed as a theoretical concern. It is also the pipeline''s most fragile stage: it depends on the nightly toolchain and an explicitly unstable JSON format, and reconciles its own identity space (rustdoc spans) against tree-sitter''s (parsed node ranges) via best-effort, multi-form file-path string matching.","non_goals":"This RFC does not propose changing the tree-sitter parsing stage, the use-statement resolver, role inference, or any query-command output format. It does not extend language support. It does not itself resolve the README-vs-CLI inconsistency over whether `rename` is deprecated (see `alternatives`) -- that needs a maintainer decision first, since question (3) above depends on it.","outcome":"Draft. Written directly from a real, verified investigation of this codebase in the same session that backfilled PDR-0001 -- not yet reviewed by a maintainer. No implementation has started.","problem":"cli/src/rustdoc_enricher.rs''s enrich_crates runs `cargo +nightly rustdoc --output-format json` once per affected crate, strictly serially (a plain for loop), even though this codebase already depends on rayon and uses it for the tree-sitter parse stage in discover.rs. Each invocation is a real subprocess: toolchain startup, analysis, and JSON serialization, not just a cache hit, even with a shared --target-dir. On a multi-crate workspace this is very likely the dominant cost of a full re-index. Separately, apply_json parses the rustdoc JSON as raw serde_json::Value rather than through the rustdoc-types crate (not a dependency here) -- it does correctly assert format_version against an EXPECTED_FORMAT_VERSION constant and fails loudly on mismatch, which is the right defensive move, but every future nightly rustdoc-json schema change still requires manually re-verifying each ad hoc .get()/.as_str()/.as_object() access chain by hand rather than getting a compiler error from a typed struct.","proposal":"Not yet settled -- this RFC exists to get the three scope questions reviewed before a PDR commits to one answer. The evidence and one plausible reading are laid out under `alternatives`.","questions_for_review":"1. Is `rename`/lsp_client.rs actively maintained and staying, or is README.md''s deprecation note the accurate forward direction and the CLI simply hasn''t caught up yet? This blocks a real answer to alternative (c). 2. Is parallelizing enrich_crates (alternative a) safe given the shared --target-dir across crates -- does cargo''s own locking make concurrent `cargo +nightly rustdoc` invocations against one target dir safe, or would parallelizing require per-crate target dirs (losing the current compiled-dependency reuse the shared dir was chosen for)? 3. Would adopting rustdoc-types (alternative b) meaningfully reduce the four-form path-matching fragility, or is that fragility inherent to reconciling any two independently-computed identity spaces regardless of how the JSON is parsed?","scope":"cli/src/rustdoc_enricher.rs and its call sites in discover.rs. Three concrete questions to resolve: (1) should enrich_crates parallelize its per-crate cargo invocations the way discover.rs already parallelizes tree-sitter parsing; (2) should apply_json''s raw serde_json::Value parsing be replaced with the rustdoc-types crate for typed, compiler-checked access; (3) should rustdoc JSON remain a separate enrichment mechanism at all, or could the LSP client already present in this codebase (lsp_client.rs, currently used for the `rename` command) absorb what rustdoc JSON currently provides -- collapsing three code-understanding mechanisms (tree-sitter, rustdoc JSON, LSP) to two."}', '2026-08-21T15:46:22.037579992+00:00', NULL, 'created');
INSERT INTO record_revision (record_id, revision, document, changed_at, changed_by, change_summary) VALUES ('RFC-0002', 2, '{"alternatives":"(a) Parallelize enrich_crates as-is (rayon .par_iter() over crates, mirroring discover.rs) -- lowest-risk, addresses the concretely-felt slowness directly, does not touch the JSON-parsing or dual-identity-space concerns. (b) Adopt the rustdoc-types crate for typed parsing -- reduces schema-drift maintenance burden, does not address serial execution or the tree-sitter/rustdoc identity-reconciliation fragility (span_file_match_keys'' four match-form normalization, four dedicated tests). (c) Investigate replacing rustdoc JSON with the existing LSP client (lsp_client.rs) as the single semantic-enrichment source -- would remove one of three code-understanding mechanisms entirely and might sidestep the identity-reconciliation problem if the LSP path can address symbols the same way tree-sitter does. RESOLVED 2026-08-21: the blocking question over whether lsp_client.rs is actively maintained or scheduled for removal is settled -- it is actively maintained. `rename` is a live, working command (main.rs Commands::Rename, dispatched through `graphlite watch`''s IPC to a persistent rust-analyzer client in lsp_client.rs), rebuilt by commit 12695cc (''feat: rename via rust-analyzer LSP through watch daemon IPC'', 2026-04-05) -- eight commits and one week after 61ec423 (2026-03-29) added the README.md line stating rename ''is no longer implemented in CLI.'' That README line is stale documentation debt, not the accurate forward direction; it was never updated after rename was rebuilt on the watch-daemon IPC path. Alternative (c) is therefore not foreclosed and is worth evaluating on its technical merits -- the open question is now purely technical, not organizational: can rustdoc JSON''s three signals (qualified_name, visibility, trait_impl/IMPL_TRAIT edges) be obtained through the same persistent rust-analyzer LSP client rename already uses, without regressing fidelity or reintroducing the identity-reconciliation fragility in a different form. These three alternatives are not mutually exclusive -- (a) and (b) are compatible with either doing or not doing (c).","constraints":"Rustdoc-derived data (qualified_name, visibility, trait_impl/IMPL_TRAIT edges) must remain available at the same fidelity for Rust projects; this is a performance/robustness/architecture redesign, not a feature reduction. Whatever replaces or restructures the current mechanism must still degrade to a per-crate warning-and-skip on failure, matching the existing failure_modes documented in PDR-0001, not a hard stop for the whole index.","motivation":"Rustdoc-based enrichment (qualified names, visibility, trait-impl edges) is the one stage of the indexing pipeline that is felt, directly and repeatedly, as slow -- reported independently by this project''s own maintainer, not just observed as a theoretical concern. It is also the pipeline''s most fragile stage: it depends on the nightly toolchain and an explicitly unstable JSON format, and reconciles its own identity space (rustdoc spans) against tree-sitter''s (parsed node ranges) via best-effort, multi-form file-path string matching.","non_goals":"This RFC does not propose changing the tree-sitter parsing stage, the use-statement resolver, role inference, or any query-command output format. It does not extend language support. It does not itself correct the stale README.md line calling `rename` deprecated (see `alternatives` -- resolved 2026-08-21: the line is factually wrong and should be fixed, but that is a one-line documentation correction independent of this RFC''s scope, not a blocker for it).","outcome":"Draft. Written directly from a real, verified investigation of this codebase in the same session that backfilled PDR-0001 -- not yet reviewed by a maintainer. No implementation has started.","problem":"cli/src/rustdoc_enricher.rs''s enrich_crates runs `cargo +nightly rustdoc --output-format json` once per affected crate, strictly serially (a plain for loop), even though this codebase already depends on rayon and uses it for the tree-sitter parse stage in discover.rs. Each invocation is a real subprocess: toolchain startup, analysis, and JSON serialization, not just a cache hit, even with a shared --target-dir. On a multi-crate workspace this is very likely the dominant cost of a full re-index. Separately, apply_json parses the rustdoc JSON as raw serde_json::Value rather than through the rustdoc-types crate (not a dependency here) -- it does correctly assert format_version against an EXPECTED_FORMAT_VERSION constant and fails loudly on mismatch, which is the right defensive move, but every future nightly rustdoc-json schema change still requires manually re-verifying each ad hoc .get()/.as_str()/.as_object() access chain by hand rather than getting a compiler error from a typed struct.","proposal":"Not yet settled -- this RFC exists to get the three scope questions reviewed before a PDR commits to one answer. The evidence and one plausible reading are laid out under `alternatives`.","questions_for_review":"1. [RESOLVED 2026-08-21] Is lsp_client.rs actively maintained, or is README.md''s deprecation note the accurate forward direction? Actively maintained: rebuilt in 12695cc (2026-04-05) to back the live `rename` command via `graphlite watch`''s IPC, a week after the README.md line (added in 61ec423, 2026-03-29) that calls it deprecated -- that line is stale, not descriptive of current intent. This unblocks alternative (c) for consideration. 2. Is parallelizing enrich_crates (alternative a) safe given the shared --target-dir across crates -- does cargo''s own locking make concurrent `cargo +nightly rustdoc` invocations against one target dir safe, or would parallelizing require per-crate target dirs (losing the current compiled-dependency reuse the shared dir was chosen for)? 3. Would adopting rustdoc-types (alternative b) meaningfully reduce the four-form path-matching fragility, or is that fragility inherent to reconciling any two independently-computed identity spaces regardless of how the JSON is parsed? 4. If alternative (c) is pursued: can the LSP client obtain qualified_name/visibility/trait_impl signals at the same fidelity rustdoc JSON provides today, and would it still support the per-crate warning-and-skip failure mode required by `constraints`?","scope":"cli/src/rustdoc_enricher.rs and its call sites in discover.rs. Three concrete questions to resolve: (1) should enrich_crates parallelize its per-crate cargo invocations the way discover.rs already parallelizes tree-sitter parsing; (2) should apply_json''s raw serde_json::Value parsing be replaced with the rustdoc-types crate for typed, compiler-checked access; (3) should rustdoc JSON remain a separate enrichment mechanism at all, or could the LSP client already present in this codebase (lsp_client.rs, currently used for the `rename` command) absorb what rustdoc JSON currently provides -- collapsing three code-understanding mechanisms (tree-sitter, rustdoc JSON, LSP) to two."}', '2026-08-21T16:05:11.116561300+00:00', NULL, 'Resolve blocking question: rename/lsp_client.rs is actively maintained, not deprecated -- README.md line is stale doc debt. Alternative (c) unblocked for review.');

INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('EDR-0001-EV-001', 'EDR-0001', 'measurement', 'Signature reconstruction drops types/return (currently inert, guard-dependent)', NULL, 'Signature-fidelity regression found during independent verification of
c1e5b96 (2026-08-21), not caught by the implementation''s own test/reporting
pass.

## What changed

extract_rustdoc_signature (src/rustdoc_enricher.rs) previously read
item["inner"]["function"]["sig"] as a raw string from rustdoc JSON. At
format_version 57 rustdoc''s `sig` field is a structured FunctionSignature
(inputs: Vec<(String, Type)>, output: Option<Type>), not a string -- so that
old `.as_str()` access was almost certainly already returning None on every
call before this refactor, making the old signature-enrichment path a
silent no-op. The new implementation reconstructs a signature by joining
only parameter *names* from `function.sig.inputs`, e.g. `fn(root,
changed_files, conn)` -- no parameter types, no return type. This is a
different kind of gap than the old dead code: it now actually writes a
value, just an incomplete one.

## Why it hasn''t caused visible damage

The apply_enrichment UPDATE only overwrites nodes.signature when the new
value is strictly longer than the existing one:
`(signature IS NULL OR LENGTH(?1) > LENGTH(signature))`. Tree-sitter''s own
signature extraction (raw source text, includes full types and return type)
is essentially always longer than the type-free `fn(a, b, c)` form, so the
rustdoc-derived signature currently never wins the comparison. Verified
directly against this repo''s own freshly-rebuilt index:
`SELECT COUNT(*) FROM nodes WHERE signature LIKE ''fn(%'' AND signature NOT
LIKE ''%:%''` returned 0 -- no node in this codebase currently carries the
degraded rustdoc-only signature form.

## Why it''s still worth fixing

The length-based guard is incidental protection, not a designed one -- it
happens to work here because tree-sitter''s signatures are verbose. Any
symbol where tree-sitter''s own extraction is missing, shorter, or absent
(a language/parse edge case, not verified to be impossible) would silently
receive the degraded type-free signature instead of a real fallback. This
mirrors the same class of issue PDR-0002 Phase 1 already fixed for
visibility (rustdoc silently overriding a value tree-sitter computed
better) -- the same treatment (either drop rustdoc as a signature source
entirely, since tree-sitter already covers it, or properly render
inputs/output types from the typed rustdoc_types::FunctionSignature) should
be applied here too, as a follow-up.', NULL);
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('EDR-0001-EV-002', 'EDR-0001', 'measurement', 'Tree-sitter signatures are full-fidelity; rustdoc signature source removed', NULL, 'Independent verification after c1e5b96 checked parser.rs::extract_signature and fresh graph queries. The parser slices each symbol''s raw source from its start through the body/signature boundary, preserving parameter types and return types. Repository samples included parse_file, exception_reason (generic lifetime and reference return), find_enclosing_fn (nested HashMap/tuple type), and send_msg_timeout. A representative temporary Rust crate additionally covered a free function, generic bounds, nested generics with closure parameters, a trait required method, and its implementation; every nodes.signature value retained full source types and return types. The refreshed repository index returned degraded=0 for signatures matching fn(...) without a colon and null_fn_signatures=0. Therefore rustdoc signature enrichment was removed entirely, matching the earlier visibility fix; no fallback or length-based signature UPDATE remains. The known e2e/clippy baseline issues remain unrelated.', NULL);
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('EDR-0001-EV-003', 'EDR-0001', 'measurement', 'CI gating completed for Phase 1', NULL, 'After the Phase 1 implementation and signature-fidelity follow-up, the repository now has a GitHub Actions CI workflow covering stable release build, strict clippy, nightly rustdoc availability, workspace-layer bootstrap, and release e2e tests. The six previously documented clippy diagnostics were fixed in commit 50da433; the exact CI commands now pass locally. The role-preference e2e case remains explicitly ignored because its expected orchestrator classification is unrelated and was already failing on c1e5b96. The next engineering decision remains Phase 2 evaluation: investigate rust-analyzer readiness and bulk semantic-sourcing reliability in a separate record; do not start it from this CI work.', NULL);
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('EDR-0002-EV-001', 'EDR-0002', 'measurement', 'Cross-crate rustdoc resolver implemented and verified', NULL, 'Implemented EDR-0002 in src/rustdoc_enricher.rs. The compute stage now retains each typed rustdoc Crate, builds a workspace-scoped qualified-name map from the upfront nodes snapshot, and resolves external trait path IDs through rustdoc paths after parallel crate parsing. Ambiguous or unresolved endpoints are skipped; existing per-crate errors and format-version bail behavior remain unchanged. Added tests/e2e.rs::workspace_discover_emits_cross_crate_impl_trait_edge with app implementing a trait defined in traits. On a representative two-crate fixture, a fresh discovery now persists AppValue -> Render alongside Document -> Render and Number -> Render. An affected-only rerun, after deleting AppValue -> Render and changing only app, recreated the edge while enriching only phase2-app, confirming the workspace map is not limited to affected crates. Verification: cargo build --release passed; cargo clippy -- -D warnings passed; cargo test --test e2e passed with 36 passed, 0 failed, 1 ignored. cargo test --lib is not applicable because graphlite has no library target.', '{"affected_only_edge_recreated":true,"build_release":"pass","cargo_test_lib":"not_applicable_no_library_target","clippy_d_warnings":"pass","e2e_failed":0,"e2e_ignored":1,"e2e_passed":36,"fresh_fixture_edges":3,"implementation":"src/rustdoc_enricher.rs","regression_test":"workspace_discover_emits_cross_crate_impl_trait_edge"}');
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('EDR-0002-EV-002', 'EDR-0002', 'measurement', 'Crate-scoped trait-name collision validation', NULL, 'Validated the accepted resolver against a three-crate workspace: trait-a::Render and trait-b::Render have identical qualified leaf names, while collision-app::AppValue implements both. Fresh discovery emitted exactly two IMPL_TRAIT edges, AppValue -> trait-a::Render and AppValue -> trait-b::Render, with each edge targeting the correct trait crate. This confirms crate-scoped rustdoc path resolution avoids same-name cross-crate collisions.', '{"collision_resolution":"pass","expected_edges":2,"observed_edges":2,"same_leaf_trait_names":true,"workspace_crates":3}');
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('EDR-0002-EV-003', 'EDR-0002', 'measurement', 'Bounded unresolved-edge diagnostics', NULL, 'Added diagnostics for non-synthetic IMPL_TRAIT endpoints that cannot be mapped. Workspace-local unresolved traits emit a scoped warning and are skipped; dependency and standard-library impls that are not indexed remain debug-level skips to avoid flooding normal discovery logs. cargo clippy -- -D warnings passed. A graphlite discovery run completed without the prior dependency warning flood, and the full cargo test --test e2e suite passed with 36 passed, 0 failed, 1 ignored. The cross-crate regression remained green.', '{"clippy_d_warnings":"pass","diagnostic_policy":"workspace-local warn, external dependency debug","e2e_failed":0,"e2e_ignored":1,"e2e_passed":36,"warning_flood_avoided":true}');
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('PDR-0003-EV-001', 'PDR-0003', 'source', 'External rust-analyzer/LSP protocol review', NULL, 'Reviewed 2026-08-21 against primary sources. rust-analyzer''s official LSP extensions document experimental/serverStatus as health plus quiescent state, describe it as persistent user-facing status, and explicitly say clients may ignore it; this supports using it as an observed readiness signal only after correlation with probe completeness, not as an unconditional correctness barrier. The official Features documentation describes workspace symbol search as fuzzy and configurable, with workspace/dependency scope and symbol-kind filtering; Configuration documents default only_types scope=workspace and a default result limit of 128. The Features documentation also confirms file structure/document symbols, hover, and go-to-implementation as supported capabilities. The LSP specification supplies the standard request/response contracts but does not provide a language-server-specific completeness or indexing-readiness guarantee. Sources: https://rust-analyzer.github.io/book/contributing/lsp-extensions.html ; https://rust-analyzer.github.io/book/features.html ; https://rust-analyzer.github.io/book/configuration ; https://microsoft.github.io/language-server-protocol/.,', NULL);
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('PDR-0003-EV-002', 'PDR-0003', 'measurement', 'Phase 2 prototype readiness and parity baseline', NULL, 'Ran the standalone read-only tools/lsp_phase2_eval.py prototype on 2026-08-21 with rust-analyzer 1.96.0 (ac68faa 2026-05-25), without modifying src/lsp_client.rs, production enrichment, or the graph database. On the representative /tmp/graphlite-signature-sample workspace, 10 fresh server launches all succeeded (10/10), each reported health=ok and quiescent=true, with first quiescence 3.762-3.799s (mean approximately 3.777s), 7 document symbols on every run, and one implementation result for the SampleTrait probe on every run. The missing-server injection produced a structured ok=false per-run result and nonzero exit. On graphlite itself, one bounded run over 24 Rust files reached health=ok/quiescent=true at 5.439s, completed in 10.858s, and returned 789 document-symbol rows; comparison against the existing graph reported 208 strict name+file-start-line matches and 313 name-only matches. The repository had no actual trait declarations matching the probe, so trait-edge behavior is evidenced by the representative sample only. These results validate a bounded repeatable transport/readiness probe, not production replacement: the harness does not yet establish corpus-wide qualified-name parity, cross-crate/blanket trait-edge parity, invalid-metadata/timeout/malformed-response injections, or rustdoc wall-time improvement. Keep rustdoc authoritative and Phase 2 in review.', '{"prototype":"tools/lsp_phase2_eval.py","repo_files":24,"repo_name_matches":313,"repo_strict_name_line_matches":208,"repo_symbols":789,"sample_document_symbols":7,"sample_first_quiescent_s_max":3.799,"sample_first_quiescent_s_min":3.762,"sample_implementation_results":1,"sample_ok":10,"sample_runs":10,"server":"rust-analyzer 1.96.0 (ac68faa 2026-05-25)"}');
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('PDR-0003-EV-003', 'PDR-0003', 'measurement', 'Multi-crate readiness, failure injection, and target-lock timing', NULL, 'Follow-up evaluation on a two-crate workspace (/tmp/graphlite-phase2-workspace) containing a path-dependent core library and app binary, with trait, generic, closure, nested-generic, private, and cross-crate-use cases. Using rust-analyzer 1.96.0 (ac68faa 2026-05-25), three fresh launches all reached health=ok/quiescent=true in 3.711-3.820s, returned 10 document-symbol rows, and returned one implementation target for the core Render trait. A copied workspace with target artifacts removed produced 10 symbols and first quiescence at 3.762s, versus 3.736-3.819s for the warm repeated launches; this is a process/workspace comparison, not a fully isolated rust-analyzer cache benchmark. Invalid Cargo metadata emitted health=error with message Failed to load workspaces and now produces ok=false. A 10ms readiness budget produces ok=false with an explicit readiness-timeout error. The same fixture timed serial cargo +nightly rustdoc package runs at 0.42s + 0.39s = 0.81s; two concurrent runs sharing one target directory printed Blocking waiting for file lock on build directory and took 0.76s wall time; two concurrent runs using per-crate target directories took 0.44s wall time. The fixture is intentionally small, so these timings demonstrate locking behavior, not production-scale speedup. LSP remains evaluation-only and rustdoc remains authoritative; complete qualified-name and trait-edge parity is still unproven.', '{"cold_quiescent_s":3.762,"invalid_metadata_ok":false,"multi_impl_results":1,"multi_ok":3,"multi_runs":3,"multi_symbols":10,"rustdoc_separate_target_parallel_wall_s":0.44,"rustdoc_serial_wall_s":0.81,"rustdoc_shared_target_parallel_wall_s":0.76,"timeout_ok":false,"warm_quiescent_s_max":3.819,"warm_quiescent_s_min":3.736,"workspace_crates":2}');
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('PDR-0003-EV-004', 'PDR-0003', 'measurement', 'Repeatability and repository regression test pass', NULL, 'Final testing pass on 2026-08-21. The two-crate representative workspace completed 10/10 fresh rust-analyzer launches successfully; elapsed times were 3.709-3.839s (mean 3.780s), first healthy quiescence was 3.706-3.836s, every run returned 10 document-symbol rows and one Render implementation result. A full graphlite repository probe over all 26 Rust files completed successfully in 11.083s, reached healthy quiescence at 5.364s, and returned 847 document-symbol rows; no trait declarations were present for implementation probing. Repository regression checks passed: cargo build --release, cargo clippy -- -D warnings, and cargo test --test e2e with 35 passed, 0 failed, 1 ignored. The ignored role-preference test is pre-existing and documented as unrelated. These results validate evaluator repeatability and no production regression, but do not establish complete LSP/rustdoc parity or justify production integration.', '{"build_release":"pass","clippy_d_warnings":"pass","e2e_failed":0,"e2e_ignored":1,"e2e_passed":35,"multi_elapsed_s_max":3.839,"multi_elapsed_s_mean":3.78,"multi_elapsed_s_min":3.709,"multi_impl_results":1,"multi_ok":10,"multi_quiescent_s_max":3.836,"multi_quiescent_s_min":3.706,"multi_runs":10,"multi_symbols":10,"repo_elapsed_s":11.083,"repo_files":26,"repo_quiescent_s":5.364,"repo_symbols":847}');
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('PDR-0003-EV-005', 'PDR-0003', 'measurement', 'Graph-backed LSP/rustdoc parity comparison', NULL, 'Ran a direct comparison against graphlite''s own rustdoc-enriched SQLite graph on the representative two-crate fixture after adding a local Number implementation, an out-of-line api.rs module, and re-exports. graphlite discover persisted two IMPL_TRAIT edges: Number -> Render and Document -> Render. Across three fresh LSP launches, textDocument/implementation on Render returned exactly two implementation locations every time; the locations were the two impl blocks and matched the graph edge inventory at the semantic implementation level. The document-symbol probe returned 15 symbols consistently. Against graph nodes, name+file-start-line matches were 12/15 and reconstructed hierarchical qualified-name matches were 9/15. The remaining exact qualified-name mismatches include rustdoc graph nodes represented as impl/type-method paths while documentSymbol exposes impl containers and fields, plus rustdoc''s private/module path normalization (for example nested::private_helper versus the stored private_helper). The out-of-line api.rs and re-exported names did not produce a transport failure, but this result is not complete qualified-name parity. Therefore LSP cannot replace rustdoc on current evidence; retain rustdoc as authoritative and keep PDR-0003 in review.', '{"fixture_cases":"generic trait impl, local concrete trait impl, out_of_line_module, reexports","impl_results_per_run":2,"lsp_impl_locations_per_run":2,"name_line_matches":12,"parity_decision":"insufficient_for_replacement","qualified_name_matches":9,"runs":3,"rustdoc_impl_edges":2,"symbols_returned":15,"workspace_crates":2}');
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('PDR-0003-EV-006', 'PDR-0003', 'measurement', 'Normalized parity and cross-crate trait-edge gap', NULL, 'Extended the parity harness with an explicit normalization layer: impl-container labels such as ''impl Render for Document<T>'' are stripped before qualified-name comparison, and private/module names are compared with suffix equivalence. On the local fixture with out-of-line api.rs, re-exports, generic and concrete impls, this raised raw qualified-name matches from 9/15 to 13/15 across three runs. Then added a local AppValue type in the dependent phase2-app crate implementing the phase2-core Render trait. LSP textDocument/implementation on the core trait returned three locations consistently: the two core impl blocks plus the app crate impl at crates/app/src/main.rs:5. The current rustdoc-backed graph persisted only two IMPL_TRAIT edges (Document -> Render and Number -> Render); the app cross-crate edge was absent. The normalized document-symbol count was 18, with 14 exact name+line matches and 16 normalized qualified-name matches, but the latter is diagnostic only because impl-container spans can alias type nodes. The cross-crate edge discrepancy is a concrete parity failure. Do not promote LSP to production sourcing yet; a future adapter must define cross-crate trait-node resolution and a non-ambiguous symbol mapping before acceptance.', '{"cross_crate_edge_in_graph":false,"cross_crate_edge_observed_by_lsp":true,"decision":"parity_failed","fixture_cases":"out_of_line_module,reexports,generic_impl,concrete_impl,cross_crate_impl","lsp_impl_results":3,"name_line_matches":14,"normalized_qualified_name_matches":16,"raw_qualified_name_matches":10,"runs":3,"rustdoc_graph_impl_edges":2,"symbols_returned":18}');
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('PDR-0003-EV-007', 'PDR-0003', 'measurement', 'Evaluation-only implementation-edge mapping', NULL, 'Added an evaluation-only mapper that resolves each LSP implementation location to the smallest enclosing graph impl node, with a source-line fallback for generic impl blocks not represented as explicit impl nodes, and resolves the probed trait declaration to its graph qualified name. On the expanded two-crate fixture, three fresh runs produced the stable LSP-mapped edge set {Document -> Render, Number -> Render, AppValue -> Render}. The rustdoc-backed graph edge set was {Document -> Render, Number -> Render}. Exact overlap was 2/3 on every run; AppValue -> Render was reported as present in LSP and missing from the graph, while no graph edge was missing from LSP. This resolves the location/type mapping question and isolates the remaining parity failure to current rustdoc enrichment''s cross-crate edge production. No production code, schema, CLI, or src/lsp_client.rs was changed. A future production design would need a deliberate cross-crate trait-path resolver or an explicitly documented limitation before using LSP as a supplement.', '{"graph_edges":2,"graph_edges_missing_from_lsp":[],"lsp_edges_missing_from_graph":["AppValue -> Render"],"lsp_mapped_edges":3,"mapped_overlap":2,"production_changes":false,"runs":3}');
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('PDR-0003-EV-008', 'PDR-0003', 'measurement', 'Rustdoc JSON cross-crate resolver prototype', NULL, 'Inspected the generated typed rustdoc JSON and added a read-only extractor to the evaluation harness. phase2_app.json contains the concrete impl at crates/app/src/main.rs:5, with target AppValue in the app crate and an external trait path entry phase2_core::Render. The extractor recovered 24 impl pairs from the two JSON documents, including AppValue -> Render, Document -> Render, and Number -> Render. The existing graph still has only the latter two IMPL_TRAIT edges because the production collect_impl_trait_edges path indexes only item IDs present in the current crate''s local rustdoc index; the external trait ID is represented in paths with a non-local crate_id and is therefore not found in item_nodes. This confirms a future resolver can recover the missing cross-crate edge from rustdoc paths, but no production resolver was implemented under PDR-0003.', '{"cross_crate_pair_recovered":["AppValue -> Render"],"graph_edges":2,"json_documents":2,"production_changes":false,"rustdoc_json_impl_pairs":24}');
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('PDR-0003-EV-009', 'PDR-0003', 'measurement', 'Post-fix LSP/rustdoc cross-crate parity', NULL, 'Re-ran the standalone LSP parity oracle after EDR-0002''s rustdoc resolver implementation on the expanded two-crate fixture with generic and concrete local impls, out-of-line module/re-exports, and a dependent AppValue implementation. Across three fresh rust-analyzer launches, all runs succeeded and returned 18 document symbols. The mapped LSP edge set was {Document -> Render, Number -> Render, AppValue -> Render}; the corrected rustdoc graph contained the same three edges on every run, with 3/3 overlap and no LSP-only or graph-only edges. Normalized qualified-name matching remained 16/18 and exact name+line matching 14/18, so this validates trait-edge parity after the fix but does not establish complete qualified-name parity or justify LSP replacement. Keep Phase 2 deferred and rustdoc authoritative.', '{"edge_overlap":3,"graph_edges":3,"graph_edges_missing_from_lsp":0,"lsp_edges_missing_from_graph":0,"lsp_mapped_edges":3,"name_line_matches":14,"normalized_qualified_name_matches":16,"phase2_decision":"deferred","runs":3,"symbols_returned":18}');
INSERT INTO evidence (id, record_id, kind, title, uri, content, metadata) VALUES ('RFC-0002-EV-001', 'RFC-0002', 'experiment', 'LSP fidelity spike vs rustdoc JSON (2026-08-21)', NULL, 'Feasibility spike for RFC-0002 alternative (c) -- replacing rustdoc JSON with
the LSP client -- run 2026-08-21 against this repo''s own graphlite crate.

## History first (context for the results below)

Commit 61ec423 (2026-03-29, "feat: add policy governance and output
budgeting") deleted the entire prior `src/lsp.rs` (1538 lines: a
multi-language rust/typescript/svelte LSP client used during `discover` for
both call-graph edges (CALLS_TRUSTED) and per-symbol enrichment via
`walk_document_symbols`), in the same commit that added `rustdoc_enricher.rs`
(qualified_name/visibility/trait_impl) and `resolver.rs` (599 lines, a new
static use-statement resolver replacing CALLS_TRUSTED with CALLS_RESOLVED).
This was a full, deliberate purge of live-LSP-during-discover in favor of
static analysis. Alternative (c) would partially reverse that.

## What was tested

Spawned `rust-analyzer` directly (no lsp_client.rs changes) against this repo
and queried `textDocument/hover`, `textDocument/documentSymbol`, and
`workspace/symbol`, then compared to the cached rustdoc JSON output at
`.graphlite/rustdoc-json/doc/graphlite.json` for the same symbols.

## Findings

1. Speed: a warm-cache `cargo +nightly rustdoc` run on this crate took 1.9s.
   rust-analyzer never emitted `experimental/serverStatus{quiescent:true}`
   within a 170s wait, but answered per-file `hover`/`documentSymbol`
   requests correctly after a fixed 25s settle -- i.e. usable before full
   quiescence, but the "fully ready" signal the codebase relies on elsewhere
   (`wait_for_ready` in lsp_client.rs) may never fire in some conditions, or
   fires much later than practical usability. Needs more investigation before
   relying on it as a readiness gate for bulk enrichment.

2. Qualified name: `textDocument/hover` on a symbol''s own name token returns
   the fully qualified module path plus the complete signature in one string,
   e.g. `"graphlite::rustdoc_enricher\n\npub fn enrich(root: &str, ...)"`.
   This works cleanly at the correct character offset. (Note: stale memory
   from this project claimed a prior hover experiment resolved 0/224 symbols
   at "definition sites" -- today''s test succeeded, so that prior failure was
   most likely a position-calculation bug, not a hover limitation. Worth
   retesting at scale before trusting either result.)

3. trait_impl: `textDocument/documentSymbol`''s outline entry name renders
   literally as `"impl Drop for LspClient"` -- trivially parseable, and
   matches what rustdoc JSON''s `inner.impl.trait.path` / `inner.impl.for`
   structured fields already give. Confirmed equivalent fidelity for this
   signal specifically.

4. Visibility -- the interesting asymmetry: `documentSymbol`''s `detail` field
   strips the `pub` keyword entirely (bare type/signature only), so
   visibility needs the heavier per-symbol `hover` call, not the cheaper
   per-file `documentSymbol` call. More importantly: tree-sitter ALREADY
   extracts a raw visibility string during the existing parallel parse stage
   that runs on every discover regardless (`extract_visibility()` in
   parser.rs:184, reading the `visibility_modifier` AST node verbatim -- e.g.
   "pub", "pub(crate)", or "private" as a fallback). `rustdoc_enricher.rs`''s
   `apply_json` unconditionally overwrites this pre-existing value with no
   NULL/empty guard (unlike its qualified_name UPDATE, which does guard).
   Comparison on `render_visibility` (a plain private fn, no modifier in
   source): tree-sitter would say "private"; rustdoc''s JSON says
   `{"restricted": {"path": "::rustdoc_enricher"}}` -- more granular for
   items with explicit `pub(in path)` restrictions, but for the common
   fully-private case neither is obviously better, and tree-sitter''s version
   is already computed for free on every discover. This suggests the
   visibility signal specifically may not need rustdoc OR LSP at all --
   worth checking as an independent simplification regardless of which
   RFC-0002 alternative is chosen.

5. Request shape: rustdoc JSON gives all three signals for an entire crate
   in one JSON blob from one subprocess call. The LSP path needs one
   `documentSymbol` call per file (cheap) plus one `hover` call per symbol
   needing visibility (O(symbols) round trips) -- at this crate''s scale
   (~30 files) this was not a problem, but the current lsp_client.rs''s
   `wait_for_id` blocks synchronously per in-flight request (built for a
   single interactive rename call, not bulk enrichment); pursuing (c) for
   real would need request pipelining, a materially different client design
   than what exists today.

## Bottom line

Alternative (c) is technically viable for qualified_name and trait_impl with
good fidelity. Visibility is the weak point -- not because LSP can''t provide
it, but because tree-sitter already provides an equivalent signal for free,
independent of this whole question. The real remaining risk for (c) is
readiness/reliability at bulk-enrichment scale (point 1 and 5), not signal
fidelity (points 2-4).', NULL);

